BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Python Essentials 1 Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T180000
UID:6ab182a5129d8e0031f40d43@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn Python and build your programming skills in a supportive
  group. This learning circle will meet on Monday evenings from November 2
  to December 7\, 4-6pm. Learning circles are study groups guided by staff
  for people who want to take a free online course together in a welcoming 
 space. Learners meet weekly and work together through the course materials
 . Participants are expected to commit to the duration of the course and at
 tend all learning circle sessions.\nUsed by startups and tech giants like 
 Google\, Facebook\, Netflix\, and more\, Python offers you endless possibi
 lities for creating small and large-scale software projects. User-friendly
  with easy-to-read code\, Python is a great first programming language to 
 learn and requires no prior programming knowledge. Python skills open you 
 up to careers in almost any industry and are required if you want to conti
 nue to more advanced\, higher paying software development and engineering 
 roles such as software engineer\, systems administrator\, and security eng
 ineer.\nPython Essentials 1\nIn this course\, you will learn in-demand ski
 lls such as how to design\, develop\, and improve computer programs\, meth
 ods for analyzing problems using programming\, programming best practices\
 , and more. The course also prepares you for the PCEP – Certified Entry-
 Level Python Programmer certification (Exam PCEP-30-0x).\nFor more informa
 tion about the Python learning circle\, please contact\nnetworkingacademy@
 tpl.ca\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this f
 orm is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries 
 Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be use
 d for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions 
 about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerli
 ne staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toron
 to ON M4W 2G8
LOCATION:Brookbanks\, Lower Level Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab182a5129d8e0031f40d43
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Career Confidence & Wellness Series: Rebounding from Rejection
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T180000
UID:6ab6cb6686d845003118c20c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Has a job rejection\, unanswered application\, or difficult in
 terview affected your confidence? We’ll explore the emotional and cognit
 ive impact of career setbacks and how to respond without letting rejection
  disarm your confidence or your next steps. You’ll learn how to identify
  what is within your control\, and reframe rejection as an opportunity to 
 learn and explore new possibilities. Through interactive activities and re
 flection\, you’ll develop strategies for self-care\, confidence\, and re
 silience.\nThis program is part of TPL's Career Coaches in Residence and i
 s generously supported by our lead donor Azrieli Foundation along with sup
 porting donors Fidelity Canada\, Friends of Toronto Public Library\, South
  Chapter and\nWilliam and Nona Heaslip Foundation.\nFor more information\,
  please visit\ntpl.ca/careercoach
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab6cb6686d845003118c20c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Author Talk: "From Insulin to Islet Transplants: Canada's ongoing 
 contribution to diabetes research" with Krista Lamb
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T190000
UID:6a8f1249b20478002995ad01@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Since Banting’s discovery of insulin at U of T in 1921\, Can
 adian scientists have remained on the front lines of the development of ne
 w treatments for diabetes\, and the quest for a cure. Around the globe\, p
 eople with diabetes are benefiting from breakthroughs with a Canadian conn
 ection.\nAuthor Krista Lamb (\nBeyond Banting\n) shares stories about the 
 remarkable scientists from across the country who are building on Banting
 ’s legacy.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on 
 this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libr
 aries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only 
 be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Ques
 tions about how the library handles your information can be directed to An
 swerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\,
  Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Davenport\, Second Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8f1249b20478002995ad01
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
